WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350
(dramatic music)

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:05.190
- On July 19, 1989,
United Airlines Flight 232

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.390
takes off from Denver, Colorado
heading towards Chicago.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.700
It's been nearly an hour since departure

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.010
and the plane is now flying over Iowa

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.493
with a sunny, clear sky and
relatively no turbulence.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.610
By all accounts, it's a routine flight

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.500
with two very experienced pilots

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.783
and one flight engineer in the cockpit.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.630
The plane is on autopilot

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.173
and is now doing a turn towards
a heading of 95 degrees.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.011
But then suddenly, with a violent jolt,

00:00:36.011 --> 00:00:37.443
everything changes.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.040
The plane is a DC-10

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.620
and it is outfitted with three engines,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.180
one underneath each wing

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.490
and a third at the base of the tail fin

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.023
towards the back of the plane.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.530
After hearing and feeling
the explosive bang,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.660
flight engineer Dudley Dvorak reports

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.450
that it is the rear
engine that has failed.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.600
Copilot William Records
takes control of the plane

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.220
as Captain Alfred Haynes

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.799
begins an engine shutdown checklist.

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:07.170
(pensive music)

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.960
Though it is alarming to
lose an engine mid flight,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.780
it isn't the worst case scenario.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.910
The DC-10 is designed to still be able

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.750
to fly even if only two
engines are operational,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.970
and so the crew is able to cut off power

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.050
to the damaged engine

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.233
and turn their attention to
steadying the plane back out.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.110
But this is when they realize

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.670
just how bad the situation actually is

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.770
because even though the plane
is able to continue flying,

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.240
Copilot Records reports

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.150
that none of the controls are working.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.390
Captain Haynes attempts to
manipulate the controls as well

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.393
and confirms that nothing
seems to be functional.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.690
While the two pilots continue flying

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.640
to see why the plane isn't responding

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.790
to the controls, Flight
Engineer Dvorak comes up

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.500
with their answer.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.178
The plane has lost all hydraulic fluid.

00:01:59.178 --> 00:02:00.840
(dramatic music)

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.430
Hydraulic fluid is needed

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.460
for nearly every control
output on the plane.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.430
For example, in order to make
a plane bank to the left,

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:11.520
a pilot will turn the
control column to the left,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.190
causing the left aileron to move up

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.340
and the right aileron to move down.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.070
The hydraulic fluid is
a pressurized liquid

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.770
that allows these ailerons
to move up and down

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.080
once the control column is moved.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.360
If there is no fluid, there's no pressure

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.980
that forces this movement,

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.080
no matter how hard the control column

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.493
is turned left or right.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.150
Now, because hydraulic
pressure is so vital

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.260
to flying a plane,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.930
there are actually three
separate hydraulic lines

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.300
that run through a DC-10.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.600
These hydraulic systems are
independent from one another

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.480
so that if one should
fail, there are two others

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.973
that can continue
providing hydraulic power.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.620
There is no procedure to
follow should all three fail

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.720
because the likelihood of
this happening was thought

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.053
to be one in a billion,
virtually impossible.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.170
And with no hydraulic fluid left,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.440
the plane begins banking to the right.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.960
If this continues, the plane is in danger

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.940
of fully rolling over,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.720
a situation that there's
no recovering from.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.230
Flight 232 will crash.
(dramatic music)

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.570
Thinking quickly, Captain Hayes realizes

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.550
that even without hydraulic fluid,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.860
they still have at least one control

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.923
that they can manipulate:
the engine throttles.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.180
By decreasing the power
to the left engine,

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.476
Haynes is able to slowly
level the plane back out.

00:03:36.476 --> 00:03:38.490
(dramatic music continues)

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.830
And with the immediate danger addressed,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.730
the crew can now turn their
attention to the bigger issue:

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.367
they have no way to fly this plane.

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:50.610
(suspenseful music)

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.620
Now, what the flight crew don't realize

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.200
is that even though all
three hydraulic lines

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.690
run separately, there is one section

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:58.740
in the back of the plane

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:02.283
where they come within close
proximity near the rear engine.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.810
Later investigations will
reveal that within the engine,

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.300
a fan disc had been produced using metal

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.490
that was not completely pure

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:15.390
and this manufacturing
defect went unnoticed.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:18.810
A crack began to form with
each flight the aircraft took

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:22.080
until finally the disc
couldn't hold anymore

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.480
and broke apart within the engine.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.540
The resulting explosion
severed the hydraulic lines

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.130
to this rear engine,

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:32.880
but it also sent shrapnel
flying all around the area

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.260
towards the same section

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.740
where the other two hydraulic
lines come close together,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.990
severing them as well.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.140
The result was all lines
bleeding hydraulic fluid

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.113
until they were empty, leaving
the flight crew helpless.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.880
At this very moment, a flight
attendant comes to the cockpit

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.340
to get an update on the situation.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.710
She can immediately
tell things aren't good,

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.870
and this is confirmed
when Haynes tells her

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:01.050
to ready the cabin for
an emergency landing.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.090
Once she returns and begins instructing

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.420
the other flight attendants
to follow suit, she is stopped

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:10.470
by a passenger sitting near
the back of first class.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:14.130
He asks for information
about what is going on

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.493
and he seems relatively
calm given the situation.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.230
That is because he is
no ordinary passenger.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:26.700
His name is Dennis Fitch
and he himself is a pilot,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.830
but not just any pilot.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.250
Fitch is actually a flight
instructor for the DC-10,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:34.533
the very aircraft that they are onboard.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.140
The flight attendant tells him

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.150
that the rear engine has shut down

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.590
and that the captain has reported

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.813
that all hydraulics are lost.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.630
Now, Fitch can't believe what he's hearing

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.060
as he knows this aircraft in and out

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.380
and understands just how
unlikely that could be.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.450
And yet if it is true, he also knows

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.550
that they are almost certainly doomed

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.250
because he has studied a previous flight

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.893
that has had this exact same problem.

00:06:02.938 --> 00:06:07.050
(airplane whooshing)
(dramatic music)

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.260
Just four years before
United Flight 232 departed

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.490
on its fated flight, Japan
Airlines Flight 123 takes off

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.103
from Tokyo en route to Osaka.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.660
It'll be a short flight, about one hour,

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.653
but nonetheless, the journey
will be made on a Boeing 747.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.550
These aircraft are known
for their large size

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.240
and are more typically used
for long distance flights.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.300
However, this particular 747 variant

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.450
is one specifically
requested by Japan Airlines

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.630
in response to high demand
for certain domestic routes,

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:45.720
such as between Tokyo and Osaka.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.960
With this model, fuel capacity
is reduced in exchange

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.720
for strengthening the
structure of the aircraft

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:55.560
to accommodate more frequent
takeoffs and landings.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.870
And because it will be
used for shorter flights,

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.970
the business class section is reduced

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.550
and the economy section is increased,

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.640
allowing for more seats overall.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.120
No other airplane type had a
higher capacity at the time

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.223
as this 747SR carried
a staggering 524 people

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:15.423
on this flight.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.750
But what no one on board knows

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:21.550
is that something is happening
in the back of the plane.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.140
Just a few years prior,

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.620
this particular 747 was
involved in a hard landing

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.290
that resulted in a tail strike.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:34.740
There were even some
injuries but no casualties

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.620
and the plane needed some serious repairs,

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.600
especially to the pressure bulkhead

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:40.923
in the back of the plane.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:45.570
This is a curved wall that
separates the pressurized air

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:50.220
in the cabin from non-pressurized
air, acting as a seal.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.260
Now, half of this bulkhead was damaged

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.830
during the tail strike, and
so repairs were carried out.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:08:00.540
The lower half dome of this
bulkhead was therefore replaced,

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.230
but this is where the
fix wasn't done properly.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.470
In the correct installation,
a single splice plate is used

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:10.563
to connect both the upper and
lower half of the bulkhead.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.310
This way, the loads are transmitted evenly

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.830
across three rows of rivets.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:20.610
But during the repair, instead
of using one single sheet,

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.850
the splice plate was cut into two parts.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.100
This is a big mistake.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.670
The top rivet is essentially
not carrying any load

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:33.780
as it's not even connected to
the lower bulkhead anymore.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:36.330
And so with each flight after the repair,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.940
cracks began to form around this area,

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:40.560
and every flight the plane took,

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.960
pressurizing and depressurizing the cabin,

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.780
the cracks grew worse and worse.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.550
It was only a matter of time

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.193
until the bulkhead couldn't hold anymore.

00:08:52.157 --> 00:08:53.520
(dramatic music)

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.400
Only 12 minutes into Flight 123,

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.450
all 524 occupants feel an
explosive decompression

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.240
as the pressure bulkhead fractures.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:07.050
Pressurized air rushes into
the space behind the bulkhead,

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.640
an area not designed

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.670
to handle the instantaneous
pressure change.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.520
This causes more ruptures
in the back of the plane

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.907
and the vertical tail fin
blows off the aircraft.

00:09:17.907 --> 00:09:20.275
(fin exploding)

00:09:20.275 --> 00:09:24.290
- [Flight Crew 1] Hey,
something has exploded!

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.123
Squawk 77.

00:09:25.123 --> 00:09:26.596
The gear door! Check the gears.

00:09:26.596 --> 00:09:28.316
- What?
- Check the gears!

00:09:28.316 --> 00:09:30.780
- The engine?
- Squawk 77.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:32.460
- [Host] The explosion is so sudden

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.310
that everything takes place
in less than half a second

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:39.808
and oxygen masks drop as the
cabin becomes depressurized.

00:09:39.808 --> 00:09:44.310
(flight crew speaking in Japanese)

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.350
- The flight crew immediately calls out

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.420
to the Tokyo control tower

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.520
and requests authorization
to return to the airport.

00:09:52.613 --> 00:09:56.053
- [Control Tower] Tokyo, Japan Air 123,

00:09:56.053 --> 00:09:59.386
request, immediate, ah,

00:10:00.384 --> 00:10:04.064
ah, trouble, request to
return back to Haneda.

00:10:04.064 --> 00:10:06.566
Descend and maintain 220. Over.

00:10:06.566 --> 00:10:08.935
- [Captain] Roger,
approved as you requested.

00:10:08.935 --> 00:10:10.333
- Permission is granted,

00:10:10.333 --> 00:10:13.210
though now the crew realizes a problem

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.220
because just like United Flight 232,

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.190
all hydraulic lines have
been severed in the explosion

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.740
caused by shrapnel
flying out in the section

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.990
where the lines come close together.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:27.815
The plane has become unflyable.

00:10:27.815 --> 00:10:29.245
(airplane engine roaring)

00:10:29.245 --> 00:10:30.893
- [Flight Crew] All the
hydraulic systems have gone dead?

00:10:32.523 --> 00:10:34.523
But, now uncontrollable.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:37.943
- [Flight Crew] Uncontrollable,
roger, understood.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.770
- With no controls and
now a missing tail fin,

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:48.150
the plane begins dipping forward and back.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:50.553
This is called phugoid motion.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:57.020
Essentially, as the plane
descends, it gains air speed,

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:00.900
but this additional speed causes lift,

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:02.853
which makes the plane climb.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:07.080
With no control surfaces
to correct this motion,

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.360
the plane continuously rises

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.080
and dips in a wave-like pattern.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:14.340
The flight crew realizes

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.060
that the only control they still
have left is engine thrust,

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:19.920
and so they begin increasing

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.110
and decreasing the plane's four engines,

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:24.990
an attempt to steer back to towards Tokyo.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:27.060
If they want to take a right turn,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.210
they increase power on the left engines

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.000
and lower power on the right.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.760
It is an extremely difficult process,

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.350
but even still, the crew is able

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.540
to make some progress
in getting turned around

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.550
to head back to Haneda Airport,

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.460
and they are decreasing their altitude.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.240
But now they have yet another
problem looming ahead of them:

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.753
the Japanese Alps.

00:11:49.753 --> 00:11:53.033
(plane whooshing)
(warning beeping)

00:11:53.033 --> 00:11:53.866
- [Flight Crew 1] Hey,
there's a mountain ahead.

00:11:53.866 --> 00:11:56.519
- [Flight Crew 2] I know.

00:11:56.519 --> 00:11:58.594
- [Flight Crew 1] Turn
right. It's a mountain there.

00:11:58.594 --> 00:11:59.427
- [Flight Crew  2] I know, Sir.

00:11:59.427 --> 00:12:03.110
- [Flight Crew 1] Take control,
to the right, right turn!

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:04.556
- [Flight Crew 2] Right
turn, that's right?

00:12:04.556 --> 00:12:06.656
- We could hit the mountain.
- I know.

00:12:06.656 --> 00:12:08.266
- [Flight Crew 1] Right turn.

00:12:08.266 --> 00:12:11.285
(plane warning beeping)
Left turn.

00:12:11.285 --> 00:12:14.688
Put more power on. Left turn now!

00:12:14.688 --> 00:12:16.290
Left turn!
(dramatic music)

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:18.810
- With little ability
to control the plane,

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:22.830
the crew do all they can
to avoid the mountains.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.683
Ultimately, there's
nothing that can be done.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:28.170
Bracing for impact,

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:32.010
the plane crashes into a ridge
just north of Mount Mikuni

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:36.053
after nearly 30 minutes of
flying an uncontrollable plane.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.031
(plane warning beeping)

00:12:40.031 --> 00:12:43.049
- [Flight Crew 1] Nose up!

00:12:43.049 --> 00:12:47.017
Power!
(plane warning beeping)

00:12:47.017 --> 00:12:49.042
- [Airplane] Bank angle.
(plane alarms blaring)

00:12:49.042 --> 00:12:49.875
Pull up!

00:12:51.191 --> 00:12:52.024
Pull up!

00:12:53.071 --> 00:12:55.242
- [Crew Member 1] It's the end!

00:12:55.242 --> 00:12:57.551
- [Plane] Pull up.

00:12:57.551 --> 00:13:02.551
Pull up.
(plane crashing)

00:13:02.889 --> 00:13:05.556
(pensive music)

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:12.150
- Rescuers immediately
dispatch to the area.

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:14.193
But the situation is dire.

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:18.000
The 747 crashed high in the mountains,

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.610
far past the point any
road or path would reach.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.730
And with the sun taking away
the last bit of daylight,

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.050
it is virtually impossible

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:28.350
to land a helicopter near the crash site.

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:30.420
After flying over the burning debris,

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:32.700
rescuers believe it is impossible

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:34.743
that anyone could have survived.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:40.590
Nearly 14 hours after the
crash, with the rising sun,

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:43.260
rescuers finally reach the crash site

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:47.193
and here they discover
a miracle: movement.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:51.660
They find a woman alive,
though severely injured,

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:54.450
and now seeing there is
hope, the search is energized

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:57.420
and they find a child stuck in a tree.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.790
Searching more, they also find a mother

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:02.913
and her daughter alive,
trapped under debris.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:07.470
Though miraculous that
four people have survived,

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.990
it is coupled with the
devastating realization

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:14.990
that all 520 other occupants
of Flight 123 have perished.

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:18.630
And even more tragic is that
doctors would later determine

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:20.160
that more could have survived

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.220
if rescuers had reached
the crash site sooner.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:25.020
And with so many casualties,

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.820
this crash becomes the deadliest one

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:31.569
involving a single plane in
history, even until this day.

00:14:31.569 --> 00:14:34.650
(screen whooshing)
(pensive music)

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:38.940
Back on board United Flight
232, just four years later,

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.910
Flight Instructor Denny
Fitch is more aware

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:44.370
of this than any other passenger.

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:47.670
Knowing that the Japan Airlines
flight faced the same issue

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:49.470
of losing all hydraulics,

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:52.950
Fitch decides to offer his
assistance to the flight crew,

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:55.923
help that Captain Haynes readily accepts.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:00.300
- [Flight Crew 1] Very
little elevator or rudder.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:01.133
Ah, shit.

00:15:02.932 --> 00:15:03.765
- [Flight Crew 2] I'll tell you what.

00:15:03.765 --> 00:15:04.598
We'll have a beer when this is all done.

00:15:04.598 --> 00:15:05.490
- [Flight Crew 1] Well, I don't drink

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:06.600
but I'll sure as hell have one.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:09.150
Little right turns.
(dramatic music)

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.740
- [Host] Haynes explains to Fitch

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.320
that they have been using
differential engine thrust

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:14.913
to fly until this point.

00:15:16.568 --> 00:15:18.086
- [Flight Crew 1] Come on, baby.

00:15:18.086 --> 00:15:19.678
- [Flight Crew 3] What's
the hydraulic quantity?

00:15:19.678 --> 00:15:20.754
- [Flight Crew 4] Low zero.

00:15:20.754 --> 00:15:22.074
- All of them?
- All of them.

00:15:22.074 --> 00:15:23.682
- [Flight Crew 1]
Quantity, quantity is gone.

00:15:23.682 --> 00:15:26.550
- [Flight Crew 4] Yeah,
all quantity is gone.

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.530
- And Captain Haynes instructs Fitch

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:31.650
to take over so that
he and Copilot Records

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:33.810
can focus on other potential methods

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.820
to get the plane safely to the ground.

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:36.941
- [Flight Crew 2] Tell
me, yell what you want

00:15:36.941 --> 00:15:37.774
and I'll help you.

00:15:37.774 --> 00:15:40.530
- [Flight Crew 1] Right
throttle. Close one, put two up.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:43.380
- [Host] And so Fitch stands
between the two pilots,

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:45.780
a throttle in each hand, increasing

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:48.450
and decreasing each based on visual cues

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:50.940
and commands from Captain Haynes.

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:54.330
- [Flight Crew 2] United 232
heavy. I understand, sir, uh...

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.475
You can only make right turns?

00:15:56.475 --> 00:15:57.847
- [Flight Crew 1] That's affirmative.

00:15:57.847 --> 00:16:01.290
- With over 400 miles left
until they reach Chicago,

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.170
the crew realizes that
they will never make it.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:06.090
And with help from air traffic control,

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.760
they locate a closer
airport they can aim for,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:12.780
located in Sioux City,
less than 100 miles away.

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:13.830
- [Flight Crew 1] I have serious doubts

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:15.180
about making the airport.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.080
Have you got someplace near there

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.190
that we might be able to ditch?

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:21.390
Unless you get control of this airplane,

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:24.030
we're gonna put it down
wherever it happens to be.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:27.060
- He instructs the flight
attendants to prepare everyone

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.400
for a hard crash landing.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:30.233
- [Flight Crew 1] We're
gonna try and put it

00:16:30.233 --> 00:16:31.770
into Sioux City Iowa.
- Yeah.

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:34.029
- [Flight Crew 1] It's gonna
be tough, it's gonna be rough.

00:16:34.029 --> 00:16:35.352
- [Flight Attendant] So we're
going to evacuate for sure?

00:16:35.352 --> 00:16:36.420
- [Flight Crew 1] The brace.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:37.980
The brace will be the signal.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:40.350
It'll be over the PA
system. Brace, brace, brace.

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:43.336
Good luck, sweetheart.
- Thanks, you too!

00:16:43.336 --> 00:16:44.700
(tranquil music)

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:45.750
- [Host] Back in the cabin,

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:48.480
the flight attendants begin
instructing passengers

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:49.950
on the positions to maintain

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:52.200
when it's time to brace for impact.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:53.820
And as they look around,

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:56.430
they are hit with the
startling realization

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:58.923
of just how many children are on board.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.740
As luck would have it,

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:06.090
today is Children's Day,
a promotion from United

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:09.900
where children can fly for only a penny.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:13.440
There are 52 minors on Flight 232

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:16.592
and many of them are traveling alone.

00:17:16.592 --> 00:17:18.510
(tranquil music)

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.010
The flight attendants ask

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:22.320
for help from several adult passengers

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:24.120
and everyone cooperates to make sure

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:26.913
that no child is sitting by themselves.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.840
Next, they turn to the four
children who were young enough

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:34.890
to travel without a seat,
those under two years old.

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:36.270
The crew has no choice

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:38.580
but to continue following procedure

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.520
and have their parents place
their children on the floor

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:44.730
in front of them and surround
the children with pillows

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:46.203
and hope for the best.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:48.960
- [Flight Control] United 232 heavy,

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.490
you're gonna have to
widen out just slightly

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.550
to your left, sir, to
make the turn to final

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:55.680
and also it will take
you away from the city.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:56.513
- [Flight Crew 1] Whatever you do,

00:17:56.513 --> 00:17:58.343
keep us away from the city.

00:17:58.343 --> 00:17:59.760
- Back in the cockpit,

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:03.540
the flight crew is steadily
making way towards Sioux City,

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:06.660
but the plane keeps pulling to the right.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.660
And so deciding it would
be best not to fight it,

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:12.000
they corkscrew their way forward

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.460
with help from ATC providing directions

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:16.590
and heading information.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.950
They also begin dumping
as much fuel as they can,

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:23.490
trying to decrease their
weight before landing.

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:25.860
And after two right-hand circles,

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:28.800
it's time to do their first left turn

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:31.093
in order to line up with the airport.

00:18:31.093 --> 00:18:34.500
(suspenseful music)

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:36.780
But even after completing that turn,

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:38.790
the plane still keeps drifting

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:42.480
to the right, off the
path towards the airport.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:45.900
And so rather than keep
fighting it, the crew decides

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:50.312
to do another right-hand
loop to get back on track.

00:18:50.312 --> 00:18:52.560
- [Air Traffic Control]
United 232 heavy, roger.

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:53.610
The airport's currently

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:56.583
at your 1 o'clock position, 10 miles.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:18:59.970
And United 232 heavy,

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:02.524
if you can't make the airport,
sir, there is an interstate

00:19:02.524 --> 00:19:06.030
that runs north to south to
the east side of the airport.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:07.740
It's a four-lane interstate.

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:08.670
- [Flight Crew 1] We're
just passing it right now.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:10.753
We're gonna try for the airport.

00:19:10.753 --> 00:19:13.830
- Finally, about nine
miles out of Sioux City,

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:16.803
the pilots make visual
contact with the airport.

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:19.740
They've managed to make it this far,

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:22.020
but now they have the worst yet to come

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:24.060
as they still need to land.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.730
One other problem is that
without the hydraulics,

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:28.350
the flaps which allow the aircraft

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.660
to fly at much slower speeds for takeoff

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:33.600
and landing cannot be extended.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.020
They are, however, able to
manually lower the landing gear,

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:40.440
letting gravity do the work of
locking the wheels in place.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:42.540
And the extra drag from the landing gear

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:44.910
helps reduce their speed a bit,

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:47.340
but not by much as they
are still going more

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:50.013
than double the speed
of a typical landing.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:55.260
The entire airport lays open before them

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.900
with every runway available.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:00.660
Sioux City Airport has three runways,

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.180
but only two of them
were active at the time

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:07.530
as runway 2/2 had been closed
permanently a year earlier.

00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:10.050
The crew hopes to use runway 3/1,

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:12.810
therefore the closed runway 2/2 is used

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:15.060
by emergency responders to set up

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:16.770
and prepare themselves to assist

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:18.933
for what they know will be a bad landing.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:22.320
But just two minutes before a touchdown,

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:25.740
Captain Haynes realizes that
they aren't lined up at all

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.360
for runway 3/1

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:29.673
and they don't have time
to make corrections.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.100
But as luck would have it,

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:35.793
they are lined up almost
perfectly with runway 2/2.

00:20:37.062 --> 00:20:38.310
- [Air Traffic Control] United 232 heavy,

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:41.820
the wind's currently
360 at 1 1, 360 at 11.

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:43.946
You're cleared to land on any runway.

00:20:43.946 --> 00:20:44.910
(flight crew member 1 chuckling)

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:45.810
- [Flight Crew 1] You wanna be particular

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:47.377
make it a runway, huh?

00:20:47.377 --> 00:20:49.020
- [Air Traffic Control] 0 1 0 at 1 1

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:51.180
and there is a runway that's closed, sir

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:53.266
that could probably work too.

00:20:53.266 --> 00:20:56.100
It runs northeast to Southwest.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.703
Pretty well lined up on this
one, I think we will be.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.830
- [Host] Immediately responders rush

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:05.824
to clear the closed runway
and make way for the plane.

00:21:05.824 --> 00:21:06.657
- [Air Traffic Control]
Sir, that's a closed runway.

00:21:06.657 --> 00:21:07.490
That'll work, sir.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:08.820
We're getting the equipment off the runway

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:10.102
and they'll line up for that one.

00:21:10.102 --> 00:21:12.553
- [Flight Crew 1] How long is it?

00:21:12.553 --> 00:21:13.386
- [Air Traffic Control]
At the end of the runway,

00:21:13.386 --> 00:21:15.210
it's just a wide open field, sir,

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:17.010
so the length won't be a problem.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:18.990
- And with nothing left they can do,

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:20.520
everyone straps in

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:23.040
and Captain Haynes calls
out to the passengers

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.740
to brace themselves.

00:21:26.162 --> 00:21:27.458
- [Flight Crew 2] That's
what's turning ya.

00:21:27.458 --> 00:21:28.676
- OK.
- Back, Al.

00:21:28.676 --> 00:21:32.054
Left throttle, left, left,
left, left, left, left, left.

00:21:32.054 --> 00:21:32.887
(plane warning blaring)

00:21:32.887 --> 00:21:35.887
- [Plane] Pull up, pull up, pull up.

00:21:37.939 --> 00:21:39.856
- [Flight Crew 1] God!

00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:41.803
- [Helper 1] Buddy to 19,

00:21:41.803 --> 00:21:44.151
he's coming down real
fast down the south end.

00:21:44.151 --> 00:21:45.717
- [Helper 2] Okay, all
right, he's going down.

00:21:45.717 --> 00:21:48.330
There he goes.
- He's on fire.

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:50.663
(sad music)

00:21:52.503 --> 00:21:55.830
- Unfortunately, despite
the crew's best efforts,

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:59.553
the uncontrollable plane
finally gets the best of them.

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:02.010
Just above the ground,

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:04.223
the plane dips sharply to the right

00:22:04.223 --> 00:22:07.113
and the right wing strikes the ground.

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:10.920
The plane immediately
begins breaking apart

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:12.750
and ignites in a fireball

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:15.690
as the wreckage continues
skidding down the runway

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:17.973
and into nearby cornfields.

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:21.540
Emergency responders rush to the scene

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:24.630
and everyone fears it'll
be just like Japan Airlines

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:26.100
all over again.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:28.020
And yet, as they approach,

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:30.933
passengers begin streaming
out of the wreckage.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:33.840
Responders begin evacuating

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:36.870
and attending to any
survivors they can find,

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:40.890
and survivors themselves
also assist each other.

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:43.200
One man escapes the burning wreckage

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:47.550
only to hear a baby crying
from somewhere in the plane.

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:50.580
Risking his own life, he goes back in

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:53.910
and finds a baby, which
somehow during the impact

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:56.790
became trapped in an overhead bin.

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:59.430
He is able to escape with the child,

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:01.863
reuniting her back with her mother.

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:07.050
And outside responders also
attempt to locate the cockpit,

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:09.363
which broke apart during the impact.

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:12.720
It takes them 40 minutes,

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:16.290
but finally they discover its
broken and charred remains

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:19.470
after seeing Flight Engineer
Dvorak waving his arms

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:20.820
from the wreckage.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:24.420
Captain Haynes, Copilot
Records, Flight Engineer Dvorak

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.810
and Flight Instructor Fitch are all alive,

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:30.300
though in critical condition.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.130
They are freed from the wreckage

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:35.070
and join the other survivors
who are now being raced

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:36.843
to the nearby trauma center.

00:23:37.888 --> 00:23:41.100
(tranquil hopeful music)

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:45.300
By the end of the rescue
efforts, it's a bitter moment.

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:50.300
113 people perished after the
crash, including 11 children.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:52.950
It is a situation

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.200
that no one should have walked away from,

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:58.680
a crash that seemed impossible to survive.

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:02.550
And yet 183 people made it.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:06.120
A combination of skill from
the crew, good communication,

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:08.790
and a little bit of luck
meant that more than half

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:12.063
of Flight 232 live to see another day.

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:14.940
Now, both these crashes unfolded

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:17.100
under completely different circumstances.

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:19.710
The United Flight lost
its hydraulics over one

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:22.140
of the flattest parts of the United States

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:25.950
while Japan Airlines found
itself over mountainous terrain.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:27.900
They were flying different aircraft

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:31.350
with vastly different mass
and control characteristics.

00:24:31.350 --> 00:24:33.450
And crucially, Japan Airlines

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:36.660
had lost its vertical
stabilizer, a failure

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:40.020
that made a controlled
landing physically impossible.

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:41.370
And the fact that the crew managed

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:42.930
to keep the aircraft in the air

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:46.880
for over half an hour is
nothing short of extraordinary.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:48.330
(happy music)

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:50.730
But what these two
incidents do have in common

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:53.970
is that they reveal just
how catastrophic the loss

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:55.683
of all hydraulics can be.

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.760
And they also have
something else in common.

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:02.490
To this day, both incidents
are regarded as some

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:06.390
of the most remarkable
examples of pilot skill.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:09.930
United 232 is often cited as
one of the clearest examples

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:12.450
of crew resource management in action,

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:15.150
a captain, first officer, flight engineer,

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:18.150
and even a training captain
all working together,

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:22.135
sharing control inputs and
communicating constantly.

00:25:22.135 --> 00:25:24.060
(pensive music)

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:26.970
But aviation didn't
always operate that way.

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:29.190
And, in fact, the culture
that made that kind

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:32.853
of collaboration possible
was born out of tragedy.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:37.650
One famous example of bad
communication leading to tragedy

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:41.100
is the Tenerife disaster in 1977

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:44.190
when two 747s filled with passengers

